<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Unitarian Party</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Unitarian Party was a political faction in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a> in the decades after independence that favoured a centralised state led from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a>, liberal reforms and European-style institutions. Its leaders included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bernardino-rivadavia">Bernardino Rivadavia</a> and General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-maria-paz">José María Paz</a>. In the civil wars against the Federalists, especially under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-manuel-de-rosas">Juan Manuel de Rosas</a>, many Unitarians were killed or driven into exile, and their cause later merged into the liberal nation-building of the 1850s and 1860s.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/unitarian-party.jpg" alt="Unitarian Party" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Unitarian_Party_(Navy).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>After the May Revolution of 1810, disagreements grew between the dominant province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a>, whose partisans became known as Unitarians, and the other provinces, the Federalists, who wanted a federation of autonomous provinces. The divide was plain by the declaration of independence in 1816. The Unitarians held a liberal outlook drawn from British liberalism of the early 19th century, defending private property, free trade and foreign investment, and a central government strong enough to impose its authority on the provinces. Their leaders were intellectuals, merchants and soldiers of Buenos Aires, with some members of the interior's elites.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bernardino-rivadavia">Bernardino Rivadavia</a> was strongly influenced by the English philosopher <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jeremy-bentham">Jeremy Bentham</a>, assuring him that the laws drafted for the new nation rested on the truths of his work. Rivadavia came to argue for a single central government, and he and his allies took the Unitarian name.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Unitarians lost their hold on power after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-cepeda-1820">Battle of Cepeda</a> in 1820 and were forced into a treaty with the other provinces. Under Rivadavia's presidency of 1826 and 1827 they briefly regained control, and the Constitution of 1826 joined a central authority to considerable provincial powers, but the provincial caudillos rejected it. After Rivadavia's resignation, Juan Lavalle's troops overthrew the Federalist governor Manuel Dorrego and shot him at Navarro.</p><p>In 1829 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-manuel-de-rosas">Juan Manuel de Rosas</a> defeated Lavalle, who went into exile, and became governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a>. General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-maria-paz">José María Paz</a> formed the Unitarian League in 1830, but on 31 May 1831 the Federalists defeated it after gauchos captured its commander, and its provinces joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/federal-pact">Federal Pact</a>. From exile in neighbouring countries the Unitarians, led by Lavalle, Paz, Gregorio Aráoz de Lamadrid and others, kept up the civil war for two more decades.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/justo-jose-de-urquiza">Justo José de Urquiza</a> defeated Rosas at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-caseros">Battle of Caseros</a> on 3 February 1852. In 1853 the Autonomists of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a>, many of them former Unitarians, broke away from the Argentine Confederation, and only after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bartolome-mitre">Bartolomé Mitre</a>'s defeat at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-cepeda-1859">Battle of Cepeda</a> in 1859 did Buenos Aires accept the federal constitution of 1853. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-pavon">Battle of Pavón</a> in 1861 the constitution was amended to give Buenos Aires greater influence, and Mitre became president of a new national government. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/domingo-faustino-sarmiento">Domingo Faustino Sarmiento</a> was also counted among the party's members.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Unitarian Party” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Returning with troops who had fought in the Cisplatine War, Juan Lavalle of the <b>Unitarian Party</b> mounted a coup in Buenos Aires Province, deposing Governor Manuel Dorrego, whom he captured and killed.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-1">1 December in history</a></li><li><blockquote>During the civil wars between <b>Unitarians</b>, who wanted a centralised Argentina, and Federalists, General José María Paz took control of Córdoba in 1829.</blockquote><a href="/state/unitarian-league">Unitarian League</a></li></ul></section>
